# Appendix B — Every Console Verb (by namespace)

Motoi groups its verbs into **namespaces** — a prefix before a slash that says
what part of the machine a verb talks to. `world/step` talks to the world;
`entity/set-vel!` talks to a thing in it; `synth/play` talks to the sound chip.
This appendix is the shelf map: what each namespace is *for*, and the verbs from
it this book put in your hands.

This is the friendly map. The **exhaustive, per-verb catalog** — every verb, its
full contract, and fifteen runnable programs each — lives in the canonical
reference at `scheme/reference/`. When you need the complete signature of a verb,
go there. When you need to remember *which drawer a verb lives in*, stay here.

## The two layers of verbs

Every program in this book leaned on two layers:

1. **The language** — plain Scheme. `define`, `lambda`, `let`, `if`, `map`,
   `for-each`, `car`, `cons`, `+`, `string-append`, and the rest. These are not
   console verbs; they are the language the console speaks. The Book of Scheme is
   their home. Motoi's standard is R7RS-small plus a small set of named parts
   (SRFI-1 lists, SRFI-13 strings, SRFI-125 hash tables, SRFI-27 random).
2. **The console verbs** — the namespaced ones below. These are what make Motoi a
   *console* and not just a Scheme: the screen, the world, the synth, the memory.

### The language layer, proven

```motoi
(display (list (+ 2 3) (* 4 5) (max 1 9 4)))
(newline)
```

```
(5 20 9)
```

```motoi
(display (list (car (list 1 2 3)) (length (list 1 2 3))))
(newline)
```

```
(1 3)
```

Arithmetic and lists are the language, always on, no namespace needed. Everything
below is the console layer built on top.

## The console namespaces, as a shelf map

| Namespace | Talks to | Book chapters | Verbs the book used |
|---|---|---|---|
| *(screen)* | the framebuffer | 3 | `pset`, `pget`, `pixels-wide`, `pixels-tall`, `fb/rect` |
| `world/` | the world + tick | 5, 7, 12, 13 | `world/gravity!`, `world/spawn`, `world/count`, `world/step` |
| `entity/` | one thing in the world | 4, 7, 11, 13 | `entity/alive?`, `entity/overlaps?`, `entity/despawn!`, `entity/set-vel!` |
| `input/` | the player's hand | 6 | `input/down?` |
| `audio/` | tempo + transport | 9, 14 | `audio/tempo` |
| `synth/` | the sound chip | 8, 14 | `synth/chord`, `synth/kit`, `synth/play` |
| `cortex/` | the console's memory | 15 | `cortex/remember`, `cortex/recall` |
| *(cart)* | saving + sharing | 16 | `save-cart`, `load-cart` |

### The screen

```motoi
(display (list (pixels-wide) (pixels-tall)))
(newline)
```

```
(80 80)
```

`pixels-wide` and `pixels-tall` report the screen's size; `pset` lights a pixel,
`pget` reads one back. Chapter 3 is the walkthrough; Appendix C is the model in
full.

### The world and its entities

`world/*` verbs set up and step the shared world — gravity, spawning, the census,
and the tick. `entity/*` verbs read and write one thing inside it — is it alive,
does it overlap another, give it a velocity, remove it. Chapters 5, 7, 11, 12, and
13 build a whole game out of these two namespaces. Appendix D is the tick model.

### Input, audio, and the synth

`input/*` reads the player's buttons (Chapter 6). `audio/tempo` reports the beat's
speed (Chapter 9). `synth/*` builds and schedules sound — a note, a chord, a drum
pattern, a whole song (Chapters 8 and 14). Appendix E is the synth internals.

### Cortex — the memory

`cortex/remember` writes a fact under a name; `cortex/recall` reads it back, even
after the console has been off. Chapter 15 is the walkthrough. Appendix F covers
where that memory lives on disk.

### The cart

`save-cart` writes the whole console — screen and sound — into one named file;
`load-cart` reads one back. Chapter 16 ships a game with them. Appendix F is the
cart format.

## How to find a verb you half-remember

- **You know what it touches, not its name.** Find the namespace in the table
  above, then open that namespace's chapter or the canonical reference section.
- **You know the name, not the details.** Open `scheme/reference/` and search the
  verb — every verb has its contract and worked examples there.
- **You are at the prompt and stuck.** The console is the most honest reference.
  Type the expression and read what it answers; a wrong call tells you something
  true (see Appendix H).

